# Interview prep: questions, system design and your story (about 3h)
title "Module 23 · Interview prep"
mission "The job interview is the final boss. Your mission: practise real questions in four rounds, draw a system design, and turn your lab work into stories with the STAR method."
explain "Doing the work is half of getting hired; explaining it clearly is the other half. This module is a mock interview in four rounds of questions, a written system-design exercise and a behavioural story. A question counts as strong when your answer covers the key ideas, in your own words. After every question you see a model answer: read it, and compare. Short, concrete answers beat long vague ones."

part "PART A · How DevOps interviews work"
learn "The usual stages" "1) A recruiter or HR screen: your background and motivation. 2) A technical screen: Linux, networking, containers, Git and a small hands-on or debugging task. 3) A deeper technical round: Kubernetes, CI/CD, Terraform, cloud, monitoring. 4) System design: for example design the deployment pipeline for a web app. 5) Behavioural: tell me about a time something broke. Companies differ, but these themes repeat."
learn "How to answer" "Think aloud: say what you would check first and why. Use a simple structure: what it is, why it matters, a short example from your own lab work. It is fine to say I have not used that in production, but I practised it in a lab and this is what happened. Never bluff: interviewers can tell, and honesty plus curiosity scores well."

part "PART B · Round 1: Linux and networking (you need 5 of 8)"
qa "What happens when you type a URL into a browser and press Enter?" 'dns|resolv|look.?up|ip address|tcp|tls|handshake|http' "The browser resolves the name to an IP address with DNS, opens a TCP connection to the server, does a TLS handshake for HTTPS, sends an HTTP request, and renders the response it gets back."
qa "A server is slow. How do you investigate?" 'cpu|memory|ram|disk|load|top|iostat|log|metric|network|monitor' "Check CPU, memory, disk and network (the USE method) with tools like top, free, df and iostat, look at logs and metrics, and ask what changed recently."
qa "What is the difference between a process and a thread?" 'memory|share|lightweight|light|inside|within|separate|isolated|own' "A process has its own memory space. Threads live inside a process and share its memory, so they are lighter but need care to avoid clashes."
qa "What does chmod 755 on a file mean?" 'owner|everyone|others|group|execut|rwx|read|write|7|5' "The owner can read, write and execute (7); the group and everyone else can read and execute (5), but not write."
qa "What is the difference between TCP and UDP?" 'reliab|handshake|order|connection|guarantee|fast|lost|retransmi|stream|video|dns' "TCP is connection-based and reliable: it orders data and retransmits lost packets. UDP is connectionless and has no guarantees, but it is faster and lighter (used for DNS, video, games)."
qa "What does load average show?" 'run|queue|wait|process|minute|cpu|busy|core|demand|1.*5.*15' "The average number of processes running or waiting to run over the last 1, 5 and 15 minutes. If it is higher than the number of CPU cores, work is queueing."
qa "What is DNS, and name one record type?" 'name|ip|address|domain|translat|resolve|record|cname|mx|ns' "DNS translates names into IP addresses. Record types: A (name to IPv4), CNAME (alias), MX (mail), NS (name servers), TXT (text)."
qa "How do you find which process is using port 8080?" 'ss|netstat|lsof|listen|port|tlnp|fuser' "Use ss -tlnp (or netstat -tlnp, or lsof -i :8080) to list listening sockets together with the process that owns them."
qa_need 5

part "PART C · Round 2: Containers and Kubernetes (you need 5 of 8)"
qa "What is the difference between a container and a virtual machine?" 'kernel|light|fast|small|hypervisor|isolat|share|operating|resource|start' "A VM virtualises hardware and runs a full operating system on a hypervisor. A container shares the host kernel and only isolates processes, so it starts in seconds and is much lighter."
qa "What is the difference between an image and a container?" 'template|running|instance|read.?only|layer|blueprint|class|recipe|package|build|run' "An image is a read-only template made of layers. A container is a running instance of an image with a writable layer on top."
qa "What is a Pod?" 'smallest|one or more|container|share|unit|group|wrapper|run' "The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes: one or more containers that share a network address and storage and are scheduled together."
qa "When would you use a StatefulSet instead of a Deployment?" 'stable|identity|storage|state|order|pvc|database|persist|data|name' "When pods need a stable identity and their own persistent storage, for example databases. A Deployment is for interchangeable stateless pods."
qa "What is the difference between a liveness and a readiness probe?" 'restart|traffic|ready|alive|healthy|kill|remove|endpoint|service' "Liveness asks is it alive: if it fails Kubernetes restarts the container. Readiness asks can it serve traffic: if it fails the pod is removed from the Service until it recovers."
qa "A pod is in CrashLoopBackOff. How do you debug it?" 'log|describe|previous|event|exit|oom|memory|config|image|probe|kubectl' "kubectl describe pod for events and exit codes (137 means killed, often OOM), kubectl logs --previous for the crashed run, and check the image, config, secrets and resource limits."
qa "Name the Kubernetes Service types." 'clusterip|nodeport|loadbalancer|internal|external|cluster ip|node port|load balancer|ingress' "ClusterIP (internal only, the default), NodePort (a port on every node), LoadBalancer (an external cloud load balancer). Ingress then routes HTTP to Services."
qa "How does a rolling update work, and how do you roll back?" 'gradual|one by one|replace|surge|unavailable|undo|old|new|zero|downtime|rollback|revert' "New pods are started and old ones removed gradually so the app stays available (controlled by maxUnavailable and maxSurge). Roll back with kubectl rollout undo, or in GitOps by reverting the commit."
qa_need 5

part "PART D · Round 3: CI/CD, infrastructure as code and cloud (you need 5 of 8)"
qa "What is the difference between continuous integration and continuous delivery?" 'integrat|test|automat|deliver|deploy|release|build|merge' "CI automatically builds and tests every change. Continuous delivery keeps every passing change ready to release (deployment may need a manual approval); continuous deployment releases it automatically."
qa "What is the Terraform state file for?" 'map|resource|track|real|lock|remote|secret|record|existing|current' "It records which real resources belong to your code, so Terraform can compute what to change. Keep it in a remote, locked, encrypted backend, never in git, because it can contain secrets."
qa "What is the difference between terraform plan and terraform apply?" 'preview|dry|show|change|create|execute|read|see|actually|what will|apply' "plan shows the changes Terraform WOULD make without changing anything; apply performs them. Always read the plan first."
qa "Explain blue-green and canary deployments." 'two|environment|switch|small|percent|traffic|gradual|rollback|new version|old version|users' "Blue-green runs two identical environments and switches traffic from old to new in one step, with instant rollback. Canary sends a small share of traffic to the new version first and grows it if the metrics stay healthy."
qa "What are the benefits of infrastructure as code?" 'repeat|version|review|consistent|drift|audit|code|automat|reproduc|history|git|rebuild' "Repeatable and consistent environments, changes reviewed like code, history in git, easy rebuild after a disaster, and drift detection."
qa "How should a CI pipeline handle secrets?" 'secret|vault|manager|encrypt|never|git|repo|oidc|short|environment|mask|variable' "Never in git. Use the CI platform's encrypted secrets or a secret manager, give the pipeline least privilege, prefer short-lived credentials via OIDC, and mask values in logs."
qa "What does least privilege mean in IAM?" 'least|minimum|only|need|role|policy|deny|restrict|limit|permission' "Grant only the permissions a person or service needs, for the shortest time, scoped to specific resources. It limits the damage when credentials leak."
qa "How would you design a web app for high availability in the cloud?" 'multiple|az|zone|redundan|load.?balanc|failover|replica|auto|scal|region|backup|copies' "Run several copies across multiple availability zones behind a load balancer, with health checks, autoscaling and a replicated database with automatic failover, plus tested backups."
qa_need 5

part "PART E : Round 4: SRE and security (you need 4 of 6)"
qa "Explain SLI, SLO and SLA." 'indicator|objective|agreement|target|contract|measure|metric|promise|customer|penalt' "An SLI is a measured number (for example availability). An SLO is the internal target for it (99.9%). An SLA is a contract with customers, usually with penalties if missed."
qa "What is an error budget and how is it used?" 'allow|failure|release|freez|reliab|budget|downtime|slo|spend|risk|ship' "The amount of unreliability the SLO allows (100% minus the SLO). While budget remains you ship features; when it is spent you pause features and fix reliability."
qa "What makes a postmortem blameless, and what should it contain?" 'blame|process|system|learn|action|timeline|root|improv|no one|person' "It looks for weaknesses in systems and processes, not people. It contains a summary, impact, timeline, root cause, what went well and badly, and action items with owners."
qa "What is the difference between MTTD and MTTR?" 'detect|restore|recover|repair|resolve|fix|notice' "MTTD is the mean time to detect a problem; MTTR is the mean time to recover or restore service. Good monitoring lowers MTTD, good runbooks and automation lower MTTR."
qa "What does shift-left security mean?" 'early|before|pipeline|scan|design|cheap|sooner|earlier|ci|development|test' "Moving security checks earlier in the lifecycle, into design, code review and the CI pipeline, where problems are cheaper to fix."
qa "A secret was committed to a public repository. What do you do?" 'rotat|revok|invalidat|change|reset|new (password|key|token|secret)|history|audit|scan|remove|delete|disable' "Treat it as compromised: revoke and rotate it immediately, check the audit logs for misuse, remove it from history if needed, and add secret scanning and a pre-commit hook to prevent a repeat."
qa_need 4

part "PART F : System design on paper"
learn "Design questions" "Interviewers want to see how you think, not a perfect diagram. A good structure: 1) ask about requirements (users, uptime, budget, team size); 2) sketch the flow from code to production; 3) say what runs where; 4) cover reliability (monitoring, alerts, backups, rollback); 5) cover security (secrets, least privilege, scanning); 6) mention trade-offs and what you would improve next."
task "Design a delivery pipeline and runtime" "Write playground/design.md answering: 'Design how a small team ships and runs a web app used by 10,000 people a day.' Cover as many of these as you can in your own words: source control and pull requests, CI (build, test, scan), an artifact or image registry, deployment with rollback, infrastructure as code, monitoring and alerts, secrets, and backups. Use  edit playground/design.md  ." "A strong answer mentions at least six of: repository/pull request, CI test, scan, registry, deploy, rollback, infrastructure as code, monitoring/alert, secrets, backup." <<'X'
f=playground/design.md; [ -f $f ] || exit 1; n=0; for p in 'pull request|repository|git' 'test' 'scan|vulnerab' 'registry|image' 'deploy' 'rollback|roll back' 'terraform|infrastructure as code|iac' 'monitor|alert|slo' 'secret|vault' 'backup'; do grep -qiE "$p" $f && n=$((n+1)); done; [ $n -ge 6 ]
---
printf '# Design\nRequirements: ~10k users/day, 99.5%% uptime, 3 engineers.\n## Flow\nCode lives in git; every change is a pull request with review. CI builds the image, runs tests and a vulnerability scan, and pushes the image to a registry.\n## Deploy\nGitOps: the cluster pulls the new image tag from git. A bad release is undone with a rollback (git revert).\n## Infrastructure\nAll infrastructure is Terraform (infrastructure as code) with plan reviewed in the pull request.\n## Reliability\nMonitoring with SLOs and burn-rate alerts, a runbook per alert, and nightly backups with tested restores.\n## Security\nSecrets in a secret manager, least-privilege roles, scanning in CI.\n## Next\nCanary releases and multi-region failover.\n' > playground/design.md
X

part "PART G : Your story (behavioural)"
learn "The STAR method" "Behavioural questions (tell me about a time...) are answered with a story in four parts: Situation (the context), Task (what you were responsible for), Action (what YOU did, step by step) and Result (what happened, with a number if you can). Use your lab: the game day, the crash loop, the bad release you rolled back. Say I, not we, for the actions."
art "STAR" <<'ART'
   S  Situation   one sentence of context
   T  Task        what you had to achieve
   A  Action      what YOU did (the longest part)
   R  Result      a number or a lesson learned
ART
mcq "In a STAR answer, which part should be the longest?" "Action: what you did|Situation|Task|Result" 1 "Interviewers hire for what you did, so spend most of the answer there."
task "Write one STAR story" "Write playground/star.md about a time something broke (use something from this lab). It needs four headings: Situation, Task, Action and Result, with real sentences under each (at least 60 words in total). Use  edit playground/star.md  ." "Example: Situation: during the game day the API stopped. Task: restore service. Action: I read the runbook, checked docker ps, saw the container stopped, read its logs and started it. Result: back in 4 minutes and I fixed the runbook." <<'X'
f=playground/star.md; [ -f $f ] && for h in Situation Task Action Result; do grep -qi "^#.*$h\|^\*\*$h\|^$h" $f || exit 1; done; [ "$(wc -w < $f | tr -d ' ')" -ge 60 ]
---
printf '# Situation\nDuring a practice game day the API container of our sample app stopped and the website started returning 502 errors.\n# Task\nI was on call and had to find the cause and restore the site quickly while keeping notes for the postmortem.\n# Action\nI followed the runbook: I checked the status code, saw 502 from the proxy, which told me the API behind it was down, read docker ps and the logs, started the container again and confirmed the health endpoint answered.\n# Result\nThe site was healthy again in about four minutes. Afterwards I wrote a blameless postmortem, fixed an unclear runbook step and added an alert for the API.\n' > playground/star.md
X
quiz "Name two good questions you could ask the interviewer at the end." "team|on-call|oncall|incident|deploy|stack|tool|learn|grow|monitor|culture|process|release|onboarding" "For example: How does the team handle on-call and incidents? How often do you deploy and how do changes reach production? What does the first 90 days look like for this role? How do you decide what to automate next?"

deeper "DevOps and cloud interview guide (Abhishek Veeramalla)|https://github.com/iam-veeramalla/devops-cloud-interview-guide" "DevOps interview preparation guide (Abhishek Veeramalla)|https://github.com/iam-veeramalla/devops-interview-preparation-guide"
explain "Done! Now take the mock interview: the exam below combines live troubleshooting, a design and a spoken round. Then read JOB-READY.md at the top of the repo."
